INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'ai-reskilling-jobs-redeployment-gap-20261105', 'global', 'USA',
'AI Reskilling Jobs: 92% of HR Leaders Say Staff Can Retrain — Yet Firms Still Hire Outside',
'',
'New research from Careerminds found 92 per cent of HR professionals believe their own employees could be retrained for AI roles, yet 51 per cent of their organisations still hire from outside when those roles open up.',
'',
$$<p>AI reskilling is the answer most employers believe in, and the one most of them are not using. New research published on 4 November 2026 found that 92 per cent of human resources professionals believe their own employees could be retrained for AI-related roles, yet more than half of their organisations still hire from outside when those roles open up.</p>
<p>The findings come from Careerminds, a workforce solutions provider, in a white paper called The AI Redeployment Gap. The research, carried out with Censuswide between 15 and 26 May 2026, surveyed 1,142 human resources professionals and people managers in the United States, at organisations with 500 or more employees. It describes a gap between what employers say about their people and what they actually do when AI creates new work.</p>
<h2>AI reskilling: belief is nearly universal, action is not</h2>
<p>The headline numbers are striking. Ninety-two per cent of HR professionals and 88 per cent of people managers said employees inside their organisations could be reskilled for the roles AI is creating. Yet 51 per cent of HR professionals and 47 per cent of people managers said their organisations hire externally for new AI-related positions, compared with just 42 per cent in each group who redeploy existing staff.</p>
<p>The gap is widest where AI hiring is hottest. In technology and telecom, external hiring outpaces redeployment by 15 percentage points among both groups. That is the sector where AI roles are multiplying fastest, and where existing staff are least likely to be moved into them.</p>
<p>Part of the problem is that nobody owns the decision. Sixty-three per cent of HR professionals said HR is responsible for AI workforce planning, but only 24 per cent of managers agreed. When two groups disagree that sharply about who is in charge, the default is to post a vacancy and hire from outside. The <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-agent-jobs-2965-roles-november-2026-20261104">2,965 AI agent jobs open in November 2026</a> show how strong that outside demand is.</p>
<h2>Why companies cannot see who to retrain</h2>
<p>A second problem is visibility. Forty-three per cent of HR professionals and 34 per cent of people managers said their organisations had limited visibility about who should even be reskilled. Companies hold records of job titles and reporting lines, but they rarely hold a clear map of the skills their people actually have, so managers cannot see that the person in the next team already has most of what an AI role needs.</p>
<p>That blind spot is expensive. The white paper draws on analysis from the Josh Bersin Company, based on roughly half a million hiring records, which found that hiring externally takes longer and shows lower retention than moving someone internally. An external hire needs months to learn how the organisation works, while an existing employee already knows the customers, the systems and the unwritten rules.</p>
<p>The pattern also feeds the churn the jobs market is already seeing. Companies that cut roles because of AI are now bringing some back: a Robert Half survey found 32 per cent of employers that eliminated a role because of AI later rehired for the same or a similar role, as the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-layoffs-reversed-companies-rehire-roles-20261105">AI layoffs reversal figures</a> show. Hiring outside for AI work while laying off the people who could have done it is the same mistake made twice.</p>
<h2>What the AI reskilling gap means for workers</h2>
<p>For workers, the gap is good news and bad news at once. The good news is that employers are hiring for AI roles from outside, so the door is open to anyone who can show the skills. The <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-training-jobs-software-engineers-20261104">AI training jobs open to software engineers</a> are one example of the new work, and the pay for that kind of role is often published openly on the posting itself.</p>
<p>The bad news is that staying put is no longer a safe strategy. If your employer believes you could be reskilled but still hires outside, waiting to be noticed will not work. The practical move is to name your skills in terms the hiring manager recognises: which AI tools you have used, what you built with them, and what went wrong and how you fixed it. That evidence matters more than a course certificate, because it shows judgement, which is the part of the work employers say AI cannot carry.</p>
<p>For people already in work, the survey points to a direct question worth asking a manager: what AI skills is the team short of, and what would it take to be considered for the next AI role that opens? If the answer is vague, the visibility problem is real in your organisation, and the safer path is to build the skills in the open, where a hiring manager outside the company can see them too.</p>
<p>Anyone approached about an AI role should apply through the employer's own careers page and never pay for a job, a training place or a placement. The United States Federal Trade Commission warns jobseekers never to pay for a job and to ignore unexpected job messages on WhatsApp or Telegram.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance</th><th>Details</th></tr><tr><td>Research</td><td>Careerminds white paper, The AI Redeployment Gap, published 4 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Censuswide, 15 to 26 May 2026; 1,142 US HR professionals and people managers at organisations with 500 or more employees</td></tr><tr><td>Believe staff can be reskilled for AI roles</td><td>92% of HR professionals; 88% of people managers</td></tr><tr><td>Hire externally for new AI roles</td><td>51% of HR professionals; 47% of people managers, against 42% in each group who redeploy existing staff</td></tr><tr><td>Technology and telecom</td><td>External hiring outpaces redeployment by 15 percentage points</td></tr><tr><td>Who owns AI workforce planning</td><td>63% of HR professionals say HR; only 24% of managers agree</td></tr><tr><td>Visibility gap</td><td>43% of HR professionals and 34% of managers report limited visibility about who should be reskilled</td></tr><tr><td>Cost of hiring outside</td><td>Takes longer and shows lower retention, based on analysis of roughly half a million hiring records</td></tr></table>
<p><small>Sources: PR Newswire at https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/new-research-92-of-hr-professionals-believe-employees-can-be-reskilled-for-ai-jobs-yet-external-hiring-outpaces-redeployment-302927992.html, publishing the Careerminds research on 4 November 2026; and Careerminds at https://careerminds.com/resources/redeployment-gap on the white paper's key findings.</small></p>$$,
'',
ARRAY['https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/new-research-92-of-hr-professionals-believe-employees-can-be-reskilled-for-ai-jobs-yet-external-hiring-outpaces-redeployment-302927992.html','https://careerminds.com/resources/redeployment-gap'],
NOW(), NOW(), NOW())
ON CONFLICT ("slug") DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO "Article" ("id", "slug", "category", "country", "titleEn", "titleNe", "excerptEn", "excerptNe", "bodyEn", "bodyNe", "sources", "publishedAt", "createdAt", "updatedAt") VALUES
(gen_random_uuid(), 'jobs-in-india-2027-foundit-survey-20261105', 'global', 'India',
'Jobs in India 2027: 68% of Professionals Plan a Switch as AI Optimism Rises',
'',
'Nearly seven in ten Indian professionals plan to look for a new job in 2027, according to the foundit Career Reset Survey of 9,951 professionals, with 82.1 per cent seeing artificial intelligence as an opportunity rather than a threat.',
'',
$$<p>Jobs in India in 2027 look set for a big churn. Nearly seven in ten Indian professionals plan to look for a new job next year, according to a survey of 9,951 professionals published on 4 November 2026, and most of them say artificial intelligence is an opportunity to grasp rather than a threat to fear.</p>
<p>The survey is foundit's Career Reset Survey 2027, carried out by the job and talent platform across India. It found that 68.4 per cent of respondents intend to actively explore new job opportunities in the coming year. Career growth, not pay, is the main driver, and the appetite to move stays strong even though many respondents also describe the job market as uncertain.</p>
<h2>Jobs in India 2027: why professionals want to move</h2>
<p>Career growth emerged as the biggest reason for switching jobs, cited by 40.3 per cent of respondents, ahead of higher salary at 29.7 per cent and better work-life balance at 20.3 per cent. That order matters: people are looking for a step up, not just a pay rise. When asked about their biggest career priorities, 29.6 per cent chose better work-life balance, followed by moving into a future-proof role at 27 per cent and earning significantly more at 26.4 per cent.</p>
<p>Only 3.7 per cent said they planned to stay and grow within their current organisations, while 18.1 per cent intended to stay but remain open to new opportunities. Put together, that leaves a large majority either actively looking or willing to be tempted, which is why employers should expect 2027 to be a year of heavy movement.</p>
<p>Tarun Sinha, the chief executive of foundit, said professionals are increasingly looking beyond immediate salary increases. His reading of the survey is that career growth is taking precedence over short-term financial gain, even though salary expectations remain high.</p>
<h2>Pay expectations stay high</h2>
<p>The survey found that 51.6 per cent of respondents expect a salary hike of at least 31 per cent to make a switch worthwhile. One in five expect an increase of more than 50 per cent, while 12.9 per cent said salary alone would not persuade them to move. Those are ambitious numbers, and they set up a test for employers: the people most likely to move are also the people expecting the biggest rises.</p>
<p>Confidence is high despite that tension. The survey found that 94.1 per cent of respondents are confident of finding a good job if needed. That confidence helps explain why so many are willing to look even in an uncertain market, and it also explains why employers who want to keep their best people will need more than a pay rise to do it.</p>
<h2>AI is an opportunity, and workers are acting on it</h2>
<p>Artificial intelligence is shaping how Indian professionals view their next move. The survey found that 82.1 per cent see AI as a career opportunity rather than a threat. Of those, 67 per cent are actively upgrading their skills to adapt to changing workplace requirements, while 15.2 per cent recognise the potential but have not yet taken action. Only 5.7 per cent expressed concern that AI could reduce job opportunities in their fields.</p>
<p>That optimism matches the hiring picture. <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-jobs-india-hiring-october-naukri-jobspeak-20261103">AI jobs in India kept growing through October 2026</a>, and the survey suggests the workers most likely to benefit are the ones already building AI skills. The <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-training-jobs-consultants-20261104">AI training jobs open to consultants</a> and the <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/ai-startup-jobs-13514-roles-20261104">13,514 AI startup roles open in November 2026</a> show where some of that demand sits.</p>
<p>The lesson for jobseekers is direct: the people planning to move in 2027 are also the people upskilling now. A candidate who can show real AI work, not just a course certificate, will stand out in a market where more than two-thirds of professionals are looking. The skills worth naming are the ones employers say they cannot automate: judgement, handling exceptions and managing relationships that AI mishandled.</p>
<h2>What to do next</h2>
<p>If you plan to look for a new job in 2027, start with the skills the survey points to. Pick one AI tool your field actually uses, learn it properly and build something real with it, then put that work where a hiring manager can see it. At the same time, be clear about what would make you move: the survey shows career growth matters more than pay alone, so a role with a real step up is worth more than a small rise in the same job.</p>
<p>Apply through the employer's own careers page or a known job platform, and never pay anyone for a job offer, an interview slot or a placement. Fake offers are common in high-demand markets, and a genuine employer never asks a candidate to pay. If an offer arrives through a chat message from a stranger, check it against the company's own website before replying.</p>
<table><tr><th>At a glance</th><th>Details</th></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>foundit Career Reset Survey 2027, published 4 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Respondents</td><td>9,951 professionals across India</td></tr><tr><td>Plan to look for a new job in 2027</td><td>68.4%</td></tr><tr><td>Top reason to switch</td><td>Career growth, 40.3%; higher salary, 29.7%; better work-life balance, 20.3%</td></tr><tr><td>Expect a hike of at least 31%</td><td>51.6%; one in five expect more than 50%</td></tr><tr><td>See AI as an opportunity</td><td>82.1%; 67% are actively upskilling; only 5.7% are concerned AI will cut opportunities</td></tr><tr><td>Confident of finding a good job</td><td>94.1%</td></tr><tr><td>Plan to stay and grow where they are</td><td>Only 3.7%; 18.1% will stay but remain open to offers</td></tr></table>
<p><small>Sources: CNBC TV18 at https://www.cnbctv18.com/education/68-of-indian-professionals-plan-to-look-for-new-jobs-in-2027-82-see-ai-as-opportunity-survey-20027931.htm on the foundit Career Reset Survey 2027; Business Standard at https://www.business-standard.com/finance/personal-finance/68-of-indian-professionals-to-look-for-new-jobs-51-expect-31-hike-126110400839_1.html on the pay expectations; and BusinessToday at https://www.businesstoday.in/jobs/story/7-in-10-indian-professionals-plan-job-switch-in-2027-half-seek-over-30-salary-hike-survey-565650-2026-11-04 on the detailed breakdown.</small></p>$$,
